# Day 34 - Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array

## Problem Statement
Given an integer array `nums` sorted in non-decreasing order, remove the duplicates **in-place** such that each unique element appears only once. The relative order of the elements should remain the same.

Return the number of unique elements `k`.

The first `k` elements of `nums` should contain the unique elements in sorted order. The remaining elements beyond index `k - 1` can be ignored.

## Code

class Solution {
public:
    int removeDuplicates(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();

        if (n == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        int i = 0;

        for (int j = 1; j < n; j++) {
            if (nums[j] != nums[i]) {
                i++;
                nums[i] = nums[j];
            }
        }

        return i + 1;
    }
};



## Time Complexity
**O(n)**

## Space Complexity
**O(1)**

## Approach
This solution uses the **Two Pointers** technique. Pointer `i` keeps track of the position of the last unique element, while pointer `j` traverses the array from left to right. Whenever `nums[j]` is different from `nums[i]`, a new unique element is found. The `i` pointer is advanced, and the new unique element is placed at index `i`. After processing the entire array, the first `i + 1` elements contain all the unique values in sorted order, and `i + 1` is returned as the number of unique elements.

